            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Salt Lake City to Cancun?</h3>
            <p>September and October offer the lowest fares, typically $250 to $450 round-trip. For winter escapes, book 60 to 90 days ahead and target January post-holiday. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend pricing throughout the year.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Salt Lake City to Cancun?</h3>
            <p>Delta Air Lines and Southwest Airlines both operate seasonal nonstop flights from Salt Lake City to Cancun, primarily during winter and spring break months. Outside peak season, connecting flights through Denver, Houston, or Dallas are required. Flight duration is approximately four hours on nonstop service.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a passport to visit Cancun from Salt Lake City?</h3>
            <p>Yes, a valid passport is required for all US citizens traveling to Mexico. Your passport must be valid for at least six months beyond your planned departure date. A tourist card (FMM) is provided at immigration upon arrival for stays up to 180 days.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Salt Lake City to Cancun flight?</h3>
            <p>Southwest allows two free checked bags. Most other carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds on international itineraries, plus a carry-on and personal item. Frontier charges separately for checked bags. Always verify your specific airline's policy before packing.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Cancun Airport to the Hotel Zone?</h3>
            <p>Private transfers cost $25 to $45 and reach the Hotel Zone in 20 to 30 minutes. ADO bus costs approximately $5 to downtown Cancun with connections to the Hotel Zone. Taxis should have fares negotiated in advance. Rental cars are useful for Riviera Maya exploration.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Is Cancun safe for American tourists from Utah?</h3>
            <p>Cancun's Hotel Zone and Riviera Maya are generally very safe for tourists. Exercise standard precautions: avoid isolated areas after dark, use official taxis, and keep valuables secure. The State Department advises increased caution in downtown Cancun outside tourist areas. Resort security is typically robust.</p>

            <h3>Q7: What is the best area to stay in Cancun for first-time visitors?</h3>
            <p>The Hotel Zone offers all-inclusive resorts, beaches, and nightlife within a self-contained strip. Playa del Carmen provides a walkable town atmosphere with beach clubs and Fifth Avenue shopping. Tulum suits boutique hotel seekers and wellness travelers. Downtown Cancun offers authentic local dining at lower prices.</p>

            <h3>Q8: Can I drink the tap water in Cancun?</h3>
            <p>No. Drink only bottled or purified water in Cancun and throughout the Yucatán. Avoid ice unless you are certain it was made from purified water. Most resorts and reputable restaurants use purified water, but confirm when in doubt. Bottled water is inexpensive and widely available.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Salt Lake City International for my Cancun flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from SLC. During holiday weekends and spring break peak, consider arriving three and a half hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Cancun is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must rebook passengers on the next available flight at no extra cost for weather or operational delays. Mexican consumer protection laws may provide additional compensation for significant delays. Maintain comprehensive travel insurance for accommodation and activity reimbursement protection.</p>
























